WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.029
"The Best Way to Test for Vitamin B12 Deficiency"

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:10.800
Unfortunately, many
diagnoses of B12 deficiency

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.640
are still made or dismissed

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.560
based solely
on a single measurement

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:17.360
of vitamin B12 levels
in the blood.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.280
Why is that unfortunate?

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.080
Well, first of all, there is
remarkable variability

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:23.280
between the results obtained

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:24.800
with different
commercially available

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.400
B12 blood tests.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.000
The same sample of blood

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.920
can come in at 240 pmol/L
in one lab

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:33.120
and almost double that
in another.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.240
Secondly, there is significant
day-to-day variation

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.120
of serum B12
within one individual.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.240
Here, researchers tested people
once a week for 10 weeks,

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:44.240
and the variation
in each individual

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.360
could be as much
as 100 points or more.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.320
One way B12 deficiency
can be missed

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.320
is when someone takes
a B12 supplement

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:53.520
right before getting tested,

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:54.960
so, based on their blood levels,

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.400
it looks like they have
more than enough

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.080
even if they’re actually
suffering a serious deficiency.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:02.800
On the other hand, perhaps
as many as one in five people

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.880
who test positive
for B12 deficiency

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:07.360
based on low blood B12 levels

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.440
may not actually be deficient.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:12.480
And then there are those with
symptomatic B12 deficiency

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.480
who test fine.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:16.960
As many as five percent
of patients

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:19.920
who end up being diagnosed
with vitamin B12 deficiency

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.240
have blood B12 concentrations
above a commonly used cut-off

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:25.920
of about 150 pmol/L,

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.680
and even more have abnormal
MMA or homocysteine levels,

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:32.320
which are signs
of functional B12 deficiency

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.840
even if overt symptoms
are not apparent.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:38.480
Levels of these metabolites
may not normalize

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:41.520
until our blood levels
get up to around 400 pmol/L.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.160
So, maybe the traditional
cut-off is too low.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.720
And it’s not just
lab test abnormalities,

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:48.400
those with marginal B12 levels

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.920
score worse
on hand dexterity tests,

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:55.760
suggesting they have covert
diminished nerve function.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:58.080
Most seriously, we can have

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:02.480
life-threatening B12 deficiency
with normal blood levels.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.720
Here, these two people
were misdiagnosed

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:06.640
as having a bone marrow cancer,

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:10.320
since it couldn’t be B12,
look how high their levels are.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:12.080
As many as one third of patients

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:14.960
with one of the most dreaded
outcomes of B12 deficiency,

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.680
subacute combined degeneration
of the spinal cord,

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:21.200
had normal or even elevated
levels of B12 in their blood.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:22.000
We’re not sure why,

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.040
but sometimes
the test just fails.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:26.240
Well, that’s not good.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.480
Now, if your test result
is really low,

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:30.000
like under 100 pg/mL,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.680
there’s about
a 90 percent chance

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.760
you are truly B12 deficient,

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:36.320
but if the cut-off amount
is higher like 200 pg/mL,

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:38.880
then actual B12 deficiency
could be missed

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:42.880
as much as half the time
if you aren’t showing symptoms.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:44.720
The reason
it’s so hard to pin down

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:47.680
the rate of false positives
and false negatives

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:50.880
is there’s no gold standard
test to compare it to.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.560
Some studies suggest
measuring the levels

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.760
of the biologically active
form of B12,

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.800
but a wide variation
in levels is also shown

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.000
depending, in part, on which lab
is running the blood test.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.560
Some recommend
using a combination of tests

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.000
to improve accuracy.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.160
For example, one or both
of the functional measures

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:09.360
of vitamin B12,

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:12.400
MMA, methylmalonic acid,
and homocysteine,

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.320
perhaps especially
when B12 values

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.160
are in that so-called
"grey zone"

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:18.880
between 150
and 300 pmol/L or so.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:21.280
Those functional measures
are pretty sensitive,

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:23.280
meaning if you are B12 deficient

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.440
there’s more than
a 95 percent chance

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.520
these tests will pick that up.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:29.840
But there are problems
with so-called specificity,

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.480
meaning there are other reasons
values can be abnormal,

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.200
like diminished kidney function
in the case of MMA,

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:38.480
and folate deficiency
in the case of homocysteine.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:42.080
In summary, accurate assessment
of vitamin B12 status

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.800
is problematic,
and there is no consensus

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.240
as to the best biomarker

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:48.800
or combination
of biomarkers to use.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:50.640
From a public health standpoint,

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:54.560
what if we just make sure
everyone’s getting their B12?

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:55.680
At least 25 countries

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:57.920
have mandatory
fortification of staples

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:00.320
with vitamin B12,
and that certainly helps.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:01.760
But in terms of testing,

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:04.640
if you have classic
B12 deficiency symptoms,

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.760
even if you have normal
B12 levels in your blood,

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:08.880
and even if your MMA

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:10.800
and homocysteine levels
are normal,

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:13.680
a therapeutic trial
of B12 supplementation

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:14.880
should be considered.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:16.720
Meaning, why not just try B12

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:18.560
and see
if your symptoms improve?

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:21.840
For example, if you have
neuropathy symptoms,

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.000
numbness, pins and needles,
loss of a sense of position,

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.960
or experience
a vibration sensation,

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:32.000
maybe you should try going
all out with B12 injections

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:33.680
to be absolutely sure.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:37.600
Are B12 injections necessary to
correct B12 deficiency, though?

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:39.920
Can it just be treated
with oral B12,

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:41.440
and what’s the best source?

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.933
I’ll answer all those questions next.

